=== Introduction
--- Saint Patrick Was a Baptist [title]
overline: Baptist & church history
heading: Saint Patrick Was a Baptist
sub: The Missionary Behind the Legend
note: A Ministry Essay · Jag’s House of Wews
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse.

=== The Missionary Behind the Legend
--- A Baptist missionary
lead: Patrick was a Baptist missionary sent to Ireland by the independent church in Britain to preach the gospel.
p: In his own surviving words, the Confessio, Patrick describes salvation by grace through faith with a clarity that would be at home in any evangelical pulpit today:

--- Sons of God and co-heirs of Christ
quote: “…He poured out his Holy Spirit on us in abundance, the gift and pledge of immortality, which makes the believers and the obedient into sons of God and co-heirs of Christ who is revealed… Let every tongue confess that Jesus Christ, in whom we believe and whom we await to come back to us… is Lord and God. He is judge of the living and of the dead; he rewards every person according to their deeds.” | Confessio 4

--- Such great grace
quote: “In the knowledge of this faith in the Trinity… it is right to make known the gift of God and his eternal consolation… I didn’t deserve at all that the Lord would grant such great grace, after hardships and troubles, after captivity, and after so many years among that people.” | Confessio 14–15
callout: Patrick affirms that obedience to the gospel, and receiving the gift of eternal life and sonship as co-heirs with Christ, is by the grace of God and received by faith.

--- I cannot be silent
quote: “Hence I cannot be silent… about the great benefits and the great grace which the Lord has deigned to bestow upon me in the land of my captivity… for this we can give to God in return after having been chastened by Him, to exalt and praise His wonders.” | Confessio 3

=== The New Birth: Coming to Faith
--- Reborn in God
quote: “I am very much God’s debtor, who gave me such grace that many people were reborn in God through me and afterwards confirmed… for a people just coming to the faith, whom the Lord took from the utmost parts of the earth.” | Confessio 38
p: Patrick saw the new birth—eternal life, regeneration—as coming by grace to those “coming to the faith.” Faith is the response to the gospel call.
callout: His isolation from the Augustine–Pelagius controversy meant he was notably silent on determinism, preaching instead a pure gospel of grace received by faith.

--- A stone lying deep in the mud
quote: “Before I was brought low, I was like a stone lying deep in the mud. Then he who is powerful came and in his mercy pulled me out, and lifted me up… That is why I must shout aloud in return to the Lord for such great good deeds of his… which the human mind cannot measure.” | Confessio 12
callout: In Confessio 12, Patrick echoes the same amazement at the grace of God that saves a wretch—the same sovereign grace that reaches every sinner who believes.

=== A Great Soul Winner
--- The Spirit was fervent within me
quote: “The Spirit was fervent within me… I pray to God to give me perseverance and to deign that I be a faithful witness to Him to the end of my life for my God.” | Confessio 33
lead: That same drive is what we should strive for today.

--- Emulate him
p: Patrick was a great soul winner. We should emulate him and his passion for the gospel—not the drunken revelry and excess that Catholics have attached to his legacy.
panel-warn: Green beer | Green beer and bawdy behavior are more fitting with the pagans he preached to than with those professing Christ.

--- A people of the Lord
quote: “How has it lately come about in Ireland that those who never had any knowledge of God… have now been made a people of the Lord, and are called sons of God… How many have been reborn there so as to be of our kind, I do not know…” | Confessio 41
p: It was this love of God and the people of Ireland that gave him innumerable souls won to the Lord.
callout: Reclaiming his legacy from Catholic hagiography reveals what he truly was: an inspiration to not faint in adversity or fall into the snares of the world.

--- My faith was strengthened
quote: “The love of God and His fear came to me more and more, and my faith was strengthened. Faith grew, and my spirit was moved…” | Confessio 16
callout: Patrick’s doctrine shines clearly through his writings: eternal life is by grace alone, through faith alone, in Christ alone.

=== An Independent Church, Free from Rome
--- Independent churches
lead: Stripping away the invented Catholic hagiography reveals that independent churches existed in the 4th and 5th centuries with tenuous or no ties to Rome or papal authority—and that their understanding of the gospel was the same biblical understanding held by Baptists today.

--- Scripture alone
p: Patrick, as an evangelical figure from a non-Roman British church tradition independent of papal authority, does away with the common tropes Catholics claim always existed in the church.
p: He relies on Scripture alone, emphasizes a believer’s response to the gospel, and avoids entirely Catholic elements such as intercession of saints, Mary, purgatory, falling from grace, priestly confession, and veneration of images—all completely absent from his writings, which are entirely gospel-focused.

--- No appeal to Rome
p: His Confessio shows no appeal to Rome for legitimacy, but a strong focus on personal faith and grace.
callout: His mission stands as the first documented major independent missionary enterprise in the post-apostolic era.

--- His life
lead: The most reputable biographies confirm that Patrick was British (born approximately late 4th–early 5th century), enslaved in Ireland as a youth, escaped, then trained and ordained in Britain—likely in the church his father and grandfather served as elders—and returned as a missionary around 432 AD.

--- Divinely called
p: A third-generation Christian, his patriarchal lineage carried a near-direct line to first-century Christianity.
p: He describes his authority as divinely called; he appeals to no pope or hierarchy for validation.
callout: His Confessio defends his mission against British critics who questioned his calling to the very people who had enslaved him.

=== Believer’s Baptism: No Infants in His Writings
--- No infant baptism
lead: Patrick does not describe or mention infant baptism (paedobaptism) at any point in his writings.
p: The two surviving documents universally accepted as genuinely from Patrick himself—the Confessio and the Letter to the Soldiers of Coroticus (Epistola)—contain several references to baptism, but every one of them describes the baptism of converted believers: adults or those who had come to faith through hearing the gospel and confessing their faith.

--- Teaching first, then baptizing
p: Patrick emphasizes baptism in the context of missionary preaching: teaching first, then baptizing those who believe—aligning precisely with the Great Commission, which he quotes repeatedly.
refs: Matthew 28:19-20
callout: All water baptisms Patrick mentions are of converted believers who have responded to the gospel. No babies or infants are referenced anywhere.

